    "rationale": "Iteration 1 scored 53.70, below iteration 0's 63.05. The oval fell from 89.8 to 56.9 mean score and progress fell from 0.226 to 0.138, so the change to a narrower 0.10 m track and lighter body hurt the oval. Slalom progress rose slightly (0.098 to 0.108) and the s-bend was flat. Across all three tracks every run still ended off track, with max_error at 0.062 to 0.066 m, and line_loss_events sat at about 1.0 per run. That pattern means the robot loses the line once, at about the 0.06 m off-track distance, and then leaves the track. The sensors are the likely limit: with x at ±0.03 m and 16 mm footprints, the outer sensors stop seeing tape at roughly ±0.05 m of lateral error, so the controller has no signal before the 0.06 m limit. I therefore widen the sensor span to ±0.05 m (x = 0.05, 0.025, 0, -0.025, -0.05) so tape stays visible at the off-track boundary. I also return sensor_size to 0.012 m to keep the signal sharper across the wider span, keep the forward offset at y = 0.06 m, restore wheel_track to 0.12 m, which was better on the oval, and keep mass at 0.6 kg with body_length 0.14 m. Expected result: fewer line-loss events that end runs, higher progress on all tracks, and a score above 63. Caveat: the span change alters the signal scale, so the Integrator will need to reweight the sensors. I am changing several parameters again, so the attribution will be imperfect.",
